rotect the commons over the  long term  and so those lessons can be applied by  other communities  all right a third reason for optimism  about our ability to think ahead  is that we have history and we have  imagination  and these are two factors that i think  really can expand our ability to think  about the future  in ways that we haven't before and that  can allow us to meet our obligations  to think more about in fact future  generations  so it's very specific it's it's it's not  just  using history randomly using history i  found in  in the examples and in in the research i  did for the book  and i'll give you first an example of  where i think history can actually be  very limiting and how we think about the  future  so this is uh the olympic village that  was built for the 1972  olympics in munich and for those who  don't know  this was an example of a group of people  who really thought they were thinking  ahead who believed they were doing what  was right in terms of planning for  future disaster so  the organizers of the munich olympics in  1972  hired a police psychologist this is a  job i didn't know existed until i  researched his book  um this was a guy named georg zieber who  had been sort of a double agent he would  infiltrate student groups student  protest groups  in in munich and he would also work for  the police and so then he would inform  the other  that the police was going to be at the  protest and that the  the protesters that the police were  going to be there i'm sorry the other  way around  that the protest was happening and um  and he thought that what he was doing  was avoiding  conflagrations between these two groups  by letting everyone know that they're  going to be there so then they just  expected it  um so anyway they hired this guy george  zieber  to paint scenarios of what could go  wrong at the munich olympics  and he came up with 26 scenarios  including some very mundane scenarios  like  someone gets pushed into a swimming p